			<div class="content" id="post-content-2120767">
									

<p>Fix the title format (capitalize it) and I'll edit this post later, after I've voted and read it.</p>
<p>Edit: Particular thing: (You know you should've been prepared for this, based on my username :P)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Should the radiation inside the containment chamber begin to exceed 50 rem, the air must be evacuated, stored in shielded gas canisters, and disposed of.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>REM stands for Roentgen Equivalent Man, and it's a measurement of dose rather than the amount of radiation <em>in</em> a room. Dose = particular to the organism exposed/contaminated, whereas the amount of radiation in a room is independent of the organism involved. I'd suggest using something like CPM (counts per minute) if you're going to keep that sentence the way it is.</p>
<p>Also, I don't know why you'd have to 'dispose of' the air in there if it's just going to get contaminated again. You could just ventilate it in a closed duct circuit. Now, are you talking about contamination, or are you talking about exposure? Does whatever is emitted from this actually <em>get on</em> (contaminate) stuff in the room, in which case, it would be radioactively contaminated particles rather than just rays and particles (I'm sorry for the science jargon. There's no other way to talk about the nuclear field) or is the room just <em>exposed</em> to radiation and does not <em>become</em> radioactive?</p>
<p>As far as the skip goes, it's not badly written at all, but I am neutral-voting on account that I feel like you just tacked on the fact that it doesn't conform to normal electron configuration rules/etc. just so that you wouldn't have to deal with extrapolating upon the periodic table in a logical manner.</p>
<p>Edit 2: <a href="http://archive.ahrq.gov/research/pedprep/pedfig6_7.gif">Here</a> is the best image I could find that illustrates the difference between exposure and contamination. The beest 1 evar is on my phone app, but I couldn't find the GIF online. :(</p>

