			<div class="content" id="post-content-2120802">
									

<p>Okay, thank you for your critique.</p>
<p>First of all, thank you for pointing out that I used the wrong unit. I'm getting the impression that you know more about nuclear physics than me. How many CPM do you think would be dangerous to have floating around in a room? Or, alternatively, how could I re-write the sentence to accomodate some other measurement? The whole purpose of cycling the air is twofold: one, so it doesn't pose a health risk to the Site or the D-class personnel, and two, because these are anomalous elements, and it's probably a bad idea to let them build up anyway.</p>
<p>The reason the air is pumped out, canned, and stored away is because it's not just radioactive, it has atoms and isotopes of anomalous elements in it, whose properties cannot be predicted. Some of these atoms rapidly decompose, but some are stable. As such, the current containment procedure is to keep the elements from building up to a high concentration.</p>
<p>Lastly, though I cannot argue with your neutral vote, I will say that I chose to explicitly point out that the anomalous elements do not conform to standard electron configuration rules because I didn't think extrapolating on the periodic table in a logical fashion would be as interesting. If I wanted to extrapolate logically, I'd only put elements on the end, and use the &quot;islands of stability&quot; thing as a justification. What I wanted was strictly <em>illogical</em> elements, since we are dealing with other universes. I recall reading once that Mendeleev believed there was an element lighter than hydrogen, which he called coronium. That makes no sense whatsoever, and everyone knows it, but that's just the sort of thing that this SCP deal with.</p>
<p>I hope that cleared things up, and again, thank you.</p>

