			<div class="content" id="post-content-2121345">
									

<p>Thanks for the input, but I just spent the past half hour trying to figure out dangerous levels of radiation in W/m<sup>2</sup> and I'm stumped.</p>
<p>Based on my research, I understand now that rem is inappropriate for measuring the radioactivity of the air in a room, but Bq seems to be an absolute value that one would only be able to reliably measure if the sample were completely enclosed in a container.</p>
<p>For this containment chamber, I envision it being equipped with multiple radiation sensors, in which case CPM would be an appropriate measurement. The advantage of using CPM is that it's simple, not just for my sake but for the containment MTF guys who are called in after a breach and all the scientists are dead.</p>
<p>Based on this source-</p>
<p><a href="http://modernsurvivalblog.com/nuclear/radiation-geiger-counter-the-radiation-network/">http://modernsurvivalblog.com/nuclear/radiation-geiger-counter-the-radiation-network/</a></p>
<p>-I'm going to go with 2,000 CPM. I chose this figure conservatively for two reasons: one, because the idea is to <em>not</em> let radiation build up to the point where it becomes a serious health hazard in a building where people work and/or live, and also because that irradiated air contains stable anomalous elements, whose very existence may damage the laws of chemistry. Naturally, the Foundation doesn't want those building up to an appreciable concentration.</p>
<p>I feel that this is the most reasonable figure that <em>I</em>, personally, could come up with, but if you can recommend a better figure, by all means do so. Again, thank you.</p>

							</div>

								
					
