<blockquote>
<p>Not really - if they're stable, they already behave according to laws of chemistry. x3</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not necessarily- there are actually a few hypothetical &quot;novel&quot; particles that some theorists have put together that could create atoms if physics worked slightly differently- if squarks, for instance, existed, they could be used interchangeably with quarks for building bizzaro-world versions of neutrons, electrons, and protons- and thus atoms with bizarre properties: say, hydrogen-like atoms with the mass of a lead atom, or magnetic carbon, or elements that react to gravitational fields like normal elements do to electrical fields, and vice versa.</p>
<p>And thats before you get into the whole &quot;what if there were additional fundamental forces&quot; and you get magictons that interact with aethertrons in the same way as protons and electrons. That'd give you things like atoms made of 1 proton, 1 magicton, and 2 neutrons that react like hydrogen but otherwise have the properties of helium, and magitron hydrogen that doesn't react with proton hydrogen, but does react with other magictron hydrogen atoms&#8230; it just gets weirder from there.</p>
<p>My take on this thing is that its demonstrating what chemistry looks like in an extremely alternate universe(s) by providing examples, possibly as some sort of misguided attempt to make friendly scientific contact.</p>

