			<div class="content" id="post-content-2120941">
									

<p>I am&#8230; disappointed.</p>
<p>The picture is really neat, and gave me somewhat high hopes for the article - what can I say, chemistry going to hell in a handbasket is a fun idea to play with.</p>
<p>However, what it ends up being is a not exactly interesting memetic effect, and, hmm. While I can't exactly argue with the logic of, well, having the new elements decay immediately (which is just what'd happen) , it's sort of a boring way out, and leads to internal logic problems.<br />
Specifically, how did they ever find out this is what happens? The new elements decay in negligible time because their composition is full of shit, and you can't even derive the hypothesis from reaction byproducts, because it deposits random elements out of a huge set. Besides, this doesn't even say what amounts of them are deposited&#8230;. Ultimately this reminds me of SCORPION STARE from Charlie Stross's books, except that one worked as a &quot;scientific&quot; explanation of the mythic medusae turning people and animals and whatevs into stone, while this has nothing particularly interesting going on.</p>
<p>As far as potential ways of improving this go, I leave you with <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Island_of_stability">this wikipedia article</a>. Maybe it's right, after all.</p>

							</div>

								
					
