					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kinggolem" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1662428); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1662428&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606447494" alt="KingGolem" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1662428)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kinggolem" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1662428); return false;" >KingGolem</a></span> <span class="odate time_1412028887 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">29 Sep 2014 22:14</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2121375">
									

<p>I don't know what can be done to improve the memetic effect, really, but as for the elements decaying, keep in mind that <em>most</em> of the anomalous elements decay, and that's good, because radiation is easy to deal with. The ones that don't are the problem, seeing as how they're distorting the laws of physics just to keep existing.</p>
<p>I imagine that they figured out that anomalous elements were being produced by testing samples procured at containment. When a composition test turns up an unusually high percentage of inconclusive results, that's a hint. There's also the idea that an organization that regularly consults an &quot;expanded periodic table&quot; may have more esoteric means of detecting anomalous elements.</p>
<p>Thanks for the link, but I am aware of the &quot;island of stability&quot; concept. If I were to expand the periodic table solely by adding transuranic elements, then, to me, it would just make too much <em>sense</em>. Adding elements to the left, right, and above the periodic table makes no sense whatsoever, and I believe this serves to add a horrifying absurdity, the idea that the multiverse isn't half as reasonable as we imagine.</p>
<p>Overall, I understand your point of view, but I feel that the reasons you listed are better left as is. I thought you might like an explanation, at least.</p>

							</div>

								
					
